The use of an abundant first-row transition metal justifies the costs of the MOF; in homogeneous catalyst complexes, such metals typically require sophisticated ligands that serve a similar purpose of creating a protective pocket. This is essential given the propensity of metals like Mn or Fe to form high-spin species and engage in radical cycles, which makes them prone to self-deactivation. The outstanding performance of this MOF catalyst is indeed attributed to the fact that the active site is embedded in a rigid framework. Another significant aspect is the simplicity of the design, which results in a remarkable robustness. The authors supported their findings with mechanistic investigations, both experimental and computational, which led to a thorough understanding of the events taking place inside the pore. These studies are valuable, as they make it possible to predict how modifications of the system can influence catalytic performance. Although the substrate scope of this reaction is rather limited, this work certainly represents a leap in the right direction for the use of MOFs in catalysis.
## MOF catalysts with improved / alternative selectivity
Examples where catalyst encapsulation does not necessarily improve the TON but rather helps to tune the selectivity in a way that is difficult to achieve in solution are more common but equally valuable. In such instances, the added value stems from additional (secondary) interactions between the reactants and
functional groups decorating the pore surface. Weller, Rosseinsky, and coworkers reported an elegant demonstration of how the pore microenvironment can influence the reaction pathway and control the selectivity.21 The authors used Crabtree's well-known cationic Ir hydrogenation catalyst, and immobilized it into a sulfonated variant of the chromium terephthalate MIL101. Crabtree's \([\mathrm{Ir}(\mathrm{cod})_{2}(\mathrm{PCy}_{3})(\mathrm{py})][\mathrm{PF}_{6}]\) no longer represents the state of the art, but it remains one of the most widely used commercially available catalysts for hydrogenations. Deactivation through self-association to form inactive polymetallic hydride clusters remains a major concern for this catalyst, but this can be minimized through the design of suitable ligands or the use of an appropriate counterion. In this work, the framework itself behaves like a counterion through its sulfonyl groups; it is through ionic interactions with these groups that the complex is immobilized. Once more, active-site isolation prolongs the lifetime of the catalyst. For the limited set of hydrogenation examples reported in the paper, the TON and TOF values are comparable to those of the homogeneous counterpart. What is remarkable though, is that the performance of the MOF-supported catalyst greatly surpasses the homogeneous Crabtree catalyst for the hydrogenation of allylic and homoallylic alcohols, which are prone to undergo undesired isomerization with homogeneous Ir systems.22 The authors attribute this beneficial effect to the functionalized pore surface, which can engage the hydroxy group in noncovalent interactions that prevent it from coordinating to the Ir center.
