Finally, Yaghi and coworkers took the enzyme analogy even further in an exemplary demonstration of accurate pore functionalization.32 A sequence of seven post-synthetic covalent modifications was carried out with surgical precision to graft various tripeptide sequences with catalytic activity in the pores. This unprecedented level of complexity was achieved without compromising the porosity of the material. The resulting functionalized MOF showed asymmetric induction arising from steric constraints, and a kind of catalytic activity that had previously only been observed with enzymes (Figure 9). In preliminary results, it was found that this material catalyzed a sequence-specific peptide cleavage. This cleavage does not occur with the unfunctionalized MOF, nor with the molecular analogue of the immobilized tripeptide. If this proof of concept is followed by an optimized system, as the authors suggest, this may indeed fulfill the ultimate goal outlined in the beginning of this perspective: to harness the tunability of MOFs to achieve enzyme-like complexity and catalyze reactions that are beyond the reach of molecular homogeneous catalysts. The development of similar peptide-decorated MOFs for enantiomeric resolution would then be an even more appealing prospect.
