## The importance of transitioning to rational catalyst design
The chemistry of MOFs has developed exceptionally over the past two decades. It is now possible to choose from designs that are stable across the entire breadth of the pH scale, and that tolerate moisture and air, and we understand how these properties are related to the structure of the MOF. Bearing in mind our focus on highly functionalized organic molecules, it is notable that today's MOFs are robust enough to outlive common functional groups with increasing reaction temperature. Therefore, MOFs are no longer the component of the reaction mixture that is most prone to decomposition. We have also acquired a deep understanding of framework defects; their location and density can be controlled, and to a large extent we can predict the influence they will have on the properties of a MOF.38
Earlier, when this wealth of information was unavailable, serendipity played a major contribution in the identification of viable MOF catalysts. However, the catalytic applications of these materials were generally limited to trivial organic reac
side the pore by carrying out meticulous mechanistic studies on existing MOF catalysts. This will enable us to learn more about i) MOF–substrate secondary interactions (where exactly the catalysis occurs, and how the framework interacts with the relevant species); ii) site accessibility (how these species travel through the framework, and whether they can access all the active sites); and finally, iii) catalyst deactivation (when the catalysis stops, why does this happen, and which element of the design requires further improvements).
## MOF–substrate secondary interactions
Significant efforts have been dedicated to distinguishing whether catalysis indeed takes place inside the pores or through fast leaching-redeposition (boomerang) mechanisms.39,40 It is essential that the reaction should take place inside the pores when the desired catalytic process is only feasible in confinement. When confinement is not strictly required, an alternative could be to use two-dimensional metal-organic layers or metal-organic nanosheets.41 This emerging class of exciting ultra-thin, graphene-like materials can be accessed through a bottom-up approach and also trough the exfoliation of 3D MOFs. Significant synthetic challenges still remain to obtain layers with controlled thickness in high yields, and the materials achieved thus far have been studied more thoroughly for their charge transport properties.42,43 However, considering they lack any diffusion-related constraints typically associated with MOFs but still achieve catalyst stabilization through active-site isolation, they present interesting potential solutions for catalysis and several interesting application are beginning to emerge.44 Nevertheless, when confinement remains necessary, we must take the conversation a step further, from inside or outside the framework to precisely where and how inside the framework.
An excellent example of how relevant this question can be is provided by Speybröck's mechanistic inquiry into a cross-aldol reaction to produce jasminaldehyde, catalyzed by Zr MOFs UiO-66 and UiO-66-NH2(Figure 10).45 This is a trivial reaction for which the use of a MOF is not necessarily justified. However, this work represents a prime example of how mechanistic
proceeds by an identical mechanism, and the reaction rates are similar at later stages, irrespective of whether or not the \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) group is present. The authors identified a proton transfer from the Zr-oxo cluster to the carbonyl oxygen of the adsorbed aldol product to be the rate determining step; hydrogen bonding with the amino groups had no significant role in the reaction mechanism. This is indeed a case of bifunctionality inside the pore. The metallic cluster displays Lewis acidic sites to activate the substrate, and also basic oxo species that serve to relay the protons. However, the grafted \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) groups on the linkers have little beneficial effect, and actually promote the formation of undesired imine species from the reactants. A precise understanding of the events taking place inside the pore and the way the reactants interact with the framework is crucial to designing better catalysts, and more studies like this should be carried out.
## Kinetics of substrate diffusion and adsorption
A better understanding of how to facilitate access to the active sites has direct implications for overcoming substrate-scope limitations. Powers and coworkers studied diffusion rates using a customized kinetic-isotope-effect (KIE) approach, adapted for MOF catalysis.47 The focus of this work was a Ru-catalyzed C–H amination reaction. The performance of the reaction was improved by encapsulation of the Ru active site inside a MOF, which resulted in the suppression of side reactions. The authors gained valuable insight into the details of the catalytic cycle, and they found that diffusion through the microporous network is slow compared the amination reaction itself. This knowledge can provide critical guidance for future design. The presented KIE analysis represents a useful new tool to understand the impact of substrate diffusion and it would be interesting to see applications in comparative studies on different MOF topologies.
and they found that diffusion through the microporous network is slow compared the amination reaction itself. This knowledge can provide critical guidance for future design. The presented KIE analysis represents a useful new tool to understand the impact of substrate diffusion and it would be interesting to see applications in comparative studies on different MOF topologies.
Furthermore, the Long group has shown that not only the reaction rate but also the selectivity can be manipulated by changing the pore size and hydrophobicity to increase van der Waals interactions (Figure 11).48 This is an interesting report of the
Furthermore, the Long group has shown that not only the reaction rate but also the selectivity can be manipulated by changing the pore size and hydrophobicity to increase van der Waals interactions (Figure 11).48 This is an interesting report of the modulation of selectivity through confinement that could fit well under the section discussing outstanding examples of catalytic MOFs for selectivity control (vide supra). More importantly, it also describes state-of-the-art mechanistic tools used to understand precisely the events taking place inside the pores. Systematic perturbations were applied to the system by careful variation of linker substitution patterns, and host–guest interactions of the resulting MOFs were monitored spectroscopically. It was demonstrated that strategic insertion of carefully chosen noncovalent interactions around the active center could increase the activity, selectivity, and stability of the MOF catalyst. Indeed, KIE and gas-adsorption experiments supported a strong correlation between the observed variations in reactivity
on the one hand, and the diffusion and adsorption of the reactants within the framework on the other. This thorough mechanistic study shows how predictable catalysis with MOFs can be, once the necessary knowledge has been acquired, and how the catalytic activity can be altered by structural modification of a site remote from the active site.
It is also important to note that substrate diffusion can be heavily influenced not only by the nature but also the degree of pore functionalization. Our group, in collaboration with the Ott group, has shown that an \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\)-evolution catalyst can benefit greatly from active-site isolation in a MOF. The functionalization level could be forced up to \(24\%\), which is the equivalent of 1 in 4 linkers bearing a catalytic group. Yet even under extreme conditions, a maximum of \(80{-}85\%\) of the active sites could be accessed by a reporter probe.49 Earlier, we observed a similar pattern for a nano-Pd-loaded MOF, where the highest catalytic activity was observed at intermediate loading.50 This is a general phenomenon with implications in catalyst design but also TON calculation. When it comes to catalyst loading inside the MOF, it cannot be said that less is more, but rather that intermediate is best to achieve an optimal balance between using the pore space efficiently and making sure that all active sites are accessible.
## Catalyst deactivation
Besides understanding how catalysis works inside the MOF, it is equally important when it fails to understand why this happens. Recent advances in operando spectroscopic techniques offer us the opportunity to observe deactivation pathways and learn important lessons. In a previous project, we were perplexed by the conflicting behavior shown by a nano-Pd catalyst supported in \(\mathrm{Cr}\) terephthalates MIL-101-NH\(_2\) and MIL-88B-NH\(_2\). These materials readily lost structural integrity under mild Suzuki coupling conditions (with \(\mathrm{Cs}_{2}\mathrm{CO}_{3}\) as a base), yet they remained highly active and recyclable. This was attributed to the excellent scavenging properties of the MOFs, which, even upon loss of long-range order, were able to recapture Pd faster that it could agglomerate into large, inactive aggregates. In contrast, the same materials showed excellent stability under the much harsher conditions of the related Heck coupling. Yet despite the remarkable stability of the composite, the catalytic reaction was sluggish, and the catalyst was deactivated after one run. It became clear to us that one of the most important challenges for the MOF catalysts community today is to develop better investigative tools for observing catalysis within MOFs. In collaboration with researchers at Christian-Albrechts University of Kiel and beamline scientists at PETRA III, DESY in Hamburg, Germany, a custom reactor was developed for the simultaneous acquisition
of operando X-ray absorption and diffraction data (XAS and PXRD). This set-up allows information about the catalytically active species to be correlated with changes in the structural integrity of the crystalline support. The reactor was designed to be used at synchrotron beamlines. It included an inbuilt miniaturized stirring plate, and parameters like temperature and pressure, as well as the addition of reagents, could be controlled remotely. This customized set-up enabled us to scrutinize any small variations in the coordination environment of Pd, and to plot these on a timeline against reaction conditions and framework stability when studying the Heck reaction. Further support was drawn from NMR spectroscopy and electron microscopy analysis (TEM and EDS). Combining all the collected information, we were able to map out the entire lifetime of the catalyst. The root of the problem of catalyst deactivation was revealed to be chemical in nature. The quick death of this catalyst was not due to loss of crystallinity and porosity, nor to Pd leaching. Instead, catalyst poisoning by an apparently innocent species present in the reaction mixture led to blocking of the active sites. Once this information was acquired, potential redesign solutions became immediately obvious. The simplest of these was to change the Pd precursor used for the preparation of the MOF
ing species altogether, or alternatively to carry out the operation under continuous-flow conditions, to wash them away. Moreover, upon changing the reaction conditions, we identified an unexpected second mechanism by which the catalyst can operate in the Heck reaction. Thus, the MOF catalyst shows impressive adaptive behavior. These types of in-situ studies could be applied to any MOF-supported catalytic systems, where traditional spectroscopic techniques are not sufficient.
This example and numerous others demonstrate that often catalysts under development will fail completely, even when they are one minuscule improvement away from becoming a successful design. As numerous factors have to align for catalysis within MOFs to be successful, this can easily happen. Carrying out mechanistic studies on systems that fail to deliver the desired result can offer valuable information, and can bring catalysts to life that would otherwise be abandoned.
## Conclusions
The exploratory approach to materials syntheses based on high-throughput screening has led to an abundance of exciting porous structures. These are of great fundamental interest, yet not all of them can be used in catalytic applications in organic chemistry.
The heterogenization costs can only be justified when the MOF catalyst provides excellent selectivity control and returns yields that outcompete other available catalytic systems. As long as there is no performance or cost margin, the simpler solution will always be preferred.
Nevertheless, there is extensive room for innovation, and a candid conversation about the strengths and weaknesses of MOFs will help us focus resources onto areas where MOFs can address unmet catalytic needs. Once a pertinent problem has been identified, catalyst design can begin, harnessing the power of reticular chemistry. Mechanistic studies are essential if we are to understand why catalysis fails when it does, and to understand secondary interactions between substrates and the framework that could lead to superior or different reactivity. Certainly, the vast area of late-stage functionalization of fine chemicals could benefit greatly from the input of the MOF community. Today's methods are only capable of distinguishing between innate (substrate-controlled) and guided (catalyst-controlled) reactivity. MOFs could reach beyond this, to develop microenvironment-controlled reactivity and enable a greater control over the outcome of catalytic reactions involving elaborate substrates.
Transformational catalysis with MOFs will require understanding and manipulating highly complex systems. Working towards this goal, we have all embarked on a journey that is naturally slow, and progress is incremental. But through the identification of areas where catalyst development is most relevant, we can accelerate this process significantly and reach our common destination sooner. It has already been demonstrated that with appropriate catalyst design and in-depth mechanistic investigations, MOF catalysts can outcompete homogeneous catalysts in certain reactions. We see no insurmountable obstacles to continue achieving even more ambitious catalysis in the future.
